  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAs of 130 PM Wednesday afternoon, a strong midlevel low was located \nacross west-central NM with widespread diffluence across the state. \nThe axis of diffluence overlaps with strong low-level WAA producing \nwidespread rain showers across the northern half of KS. Despite the \nlack of convective nature of the ongoing activity, PW values near 2\" \nare resulting in occasional heavy rainfall. A very pronounced dry \nslot and slightly steeper midlevel lapses are advecting into \nsouthwest and south-central KS at the moment. This is transitioning \nthe dense stratus cloud deck into more partly cloudy conditions. In \naddition, temperatures quickly warm into the upper 70s and low 80s \nacross far southern KS/northern OK resulting in the development of \n1000-1500 J/kg of surface based CAPE. This destabilization will \ncontinue to spread north and northeast into south-central and \ncentral throughout the afternoon hours. This destabilization will \noverlap with a moderately to strongly sheared environment (effective \nshear 40-50 kt). Dissecting the shear profiles reveals long, veering \nhodographs with 0-3 km storm relative helicity (SRH) values up to \n300 m2/s2. The overlap of these ingredients is increasing confidence \nin at least scattered supercell development in the next couple of \nhours. The main concern, unfortunately, is tornadoes. The axis of \ngreatest concern resides along and northwest of the KS Turnpike. \nGiven the very moist boundary layer, the boundary layer will be slow \nto destabilize into the late evening/early overnight hours and may \nallow the severe thunderstorm potential to persist into the \novernight hours. \n\nThe midlevel low and an associated speed max will eject into the \nPlains late tonight into Thursday morning. Strong height falls and \nWAA will result in widespread shower and thunderstorm activity, \nespecially across southeast KS. Antecedent conditions remain quite \ndry across southeast KS with the past 14 days rain totals generally \nless than 1\". As such, deferred on a Flood Watch for southeast KS as \n6 hour flash-flood guidance remains between 3-4\". \n\nAs the midlevel low/trough slides through the area Thursday into \nThursday night, deformation precipitation will progress through the \nentire area. Instability will remain in place with upwards of 2000 \nJ/kg of surface based CAPE across portions of central, south-central \nand southeast KS. Wind profiles become more unidirectional with \nstraight hodographs, although effective shear values up to 40 kt a \nlikely. As such, a few strong to severe thunderstorms are possible \nwith large hail, damaging winds, and heavy rainfall being the \nprimary concerns. The Flood Watch remains unchanged since last \nnight's expansion with an additional 1-3\" expected for most \nlocations. Isolated higher rainfall amounts are probable. Any \nlingering showers and thunderstorms will clear the area Friday \nmorning as the trough slides east of the area. \n\nFor Friday and beyond, midlevel heights will increase as western \nCONUS ridge axis builds into the Plains. This should yield drier \nconditions but seasonable temperatures in the 70s.",
